      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-284047",
      ":question/problem": "In an electric circuit, Ohm's law is given by the formula\n\n$I = \\frac{V}{R}$,\n\nwhere $I$ is the current in amperes, $V$ is the potential difference in volts, and $R$ is the resistance in ohms.\n\nSuppose a circuit has a resistance of $250$ ohms, and its potential difference comes from $n$ twelve-volt batteries, producing a total of $12n$ volts. If the current in the circuit must not exceed $0.5$ amperes, what is the greatest number $n$ of batteries that can be used?"
